-- CodeBettle seed data — generated from codebettle/lib/data/*.ts
-- Matches the Prisma 8 contract (src/prisma/contract.prisma).
-- HOW TO RUN: Supabase dashboard -> SQL Editor -> New query -> paste this whole file -> Run.
-- Runs inside one transaction: if any statement fails, nothing is inserted.
-- RUN ONCE. Re-running fails on duplicate keys (that is intentional).
-- To start over, run first (separate query): TRUNCATE "User","Course","Module","Lesson","Enrollment","LessonProgress","Certificate","Coupon","Announcement","Review","Testimonial","BlogPost" CASCADE;
--
-- ROWS: User 27, Course 8, Module 13, Lesson 25, Enrollment 15, LessonProgress 42,
--   Certificate 6, Coupon 4, Announcement 5, Review 6, Testimonial 6, BlogPost 4. Total 161.
-- CHECKS (verified at generation): amountPaid total = PKR 57,489; every foreign key resolves;
--   all 6 certificates belong to completed enrolments.
--
-- PASSWORDS: every seeded user gets the SAME bcrypt hash of a random value nobody
--   knows (generated at build time, plaintext discarded). No known password can
--   log in as any seeded account. This is deliberate: the old demo passwords were
--   public in the repo, so seeding them would leave the live database open.
--   Real admins set their passwords via first-run setup / password reset.
--   (Seed admin-1 'Ahmad Hassan' <admin@codebettle.com> is a DEMO account, not the
--   real CEO — the real admins are not in this seed and are created separately.)
--
-- DROPPED (no column in the contract): User.enrolledCourses + wishlist (derived from
--   Enrollment; wishlist has no table yet), Course instructor reviews,
--   Course.schedule (no schedule data in the seed). Lesson.date IS seeded —
--   the contract gained a nullable Lesson.date column (migration
--   20261109T1926_add_lesson_date) because course pages display it.
--   Course titles are preserved exactly as stored in the seed, including
--   any odd characters.
-- Certificate verificationToken values were generated randomly here (32 hex chars each).
--
BEGIN;

**1. useState** — The foundation of all state management in functional components.

**2. useEffect** — Handle side effects like API calls, subscriptions, and DOM manipulation.

**3. useContext** — Consume React context without prop drilling.

**4. useReducer** — Manage complex state logic with a reducer pattern.

**5. useMemo** — Memoize expensive computations to optimize performance.

**6. useCallback** — Memoize callback functions to prevent unnecessary re-renders.

**7. useRef** — Access DOM elements and persist values between renders without causing re-renders.

**8. useLayoutEffect** — Like useEffect, but fires synchronously after DOM mutations.

**9. useId** — Generate unique IDs for accessibility attributes.

**10. Custom Hooks** — Encapsulate reusable stateful logic for sharing across components.', NULL, 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1633356122544-f134324a6cee?w=800&q=80', 'React', 'inst-1', 'Usman Ali', '2024-05-15'::timestamptz, true, '8 min read', '{"React","Hooks","JavaScript"}', '2024-05-15'::timestamptz, '2024-05-15'::timestamptz);

